sections.s2_technical:
`Comparator.comparingInt(Employee::age)` sorts ascending by age; `thenComparing(Employee::name)` orders equal-age employees alphabetically.

```java
import java.util.Comparator;
import java.util.List;

record Employee(String name, int age) {}

Comparator<Employee> byAge = Comparator.comparingInt(Employee::age);
Comparator<Employee> byAgeThenName = byAge.thenComparing(Employee::name);
List<Employee> staff = new java.util.ArrayList<>(List.of(
    new Employee("Rajesh", 30), new Employee("Priya", 30), new Employee("Amit", 25)));
staff.sort(byAgeThenName);   // Amit(25), Priya(30), Rajesh(30)
```

The senior point: prefer the factory methods over hand-written compare blocks because they are null-safe with `nullsFirst`/`nullsLast`, read naturally, and compose cleanly for reversed or multi-key ordering.

